# TODO - Integrate architecture checks

## Objective

Make the P1 contracts a required part of the existing validator.

## Acceptance

- Run positive and negative tests without requiring original media.
- Fail closed on verified contract violations.

## Evidence

Partial evidence, 2026-11-24: the mandatory boundary gate uses Cargo's
resolved workspace membership and targets. It rejects packages outside
direct crate directories, excluded crates, orphan Rust source, separate
integration test targets (including explicit targets outside tests/), and
manifest symlinks. The root unsafe-code forbid, denied warnings and denied
unfulfilled lint expectations cannot be weakened through the manifest.

Synthetic positive and negative controls cover valid member layouts,
misplaced members, missing package metadata, explicit integration targets
and root lint weakening. The checks run without original media as part
of the existing validation command. Fresh validation passed in run
1795577195880786433-2782349 (91 workspace tests).

Network, security and performance acceptance remain open. The integrated
gate does not prove physical LAN interoperability, mod isolation, runtime
performance or gameplay parity.

The gate additionally verifies Cargo target source paths against the owning
crate and the authored-file inventory. Custom target declarations cannot
point into another crate, ignored assets or private reference storage.
Fresh validation passed in run 1795577329926246004-2812824
(94 workspace tests).

Authored-file discovery explicitly ignores personal ripgrep configuration,
so a local glob cannot hide source files from validation or fingerprints.
An isolated regression fixture supplies a configuration that would otherwise
exclude Rust files. Fresh validation passed in run
1795577633179381736-2865763.

The pre-commit gate rejects an index change when its own worktree path
has an additional unstaged modification, and rejects restored files
scheduled for deletion. Unrelated unstaged paths remain allowed for
coordinated concurrent work. The path check also covers Git type changes.
Synthetic path-list and filesystem fixtures pass in fresh run
1795577633179381736-2865763. This check does not constitute a separate
validation build of an isolated index snapshot.

The authored-file inventory now cross-checks tracked and nonignored
untracked Git paths against ripgrep discovery. It rejects source symlinks,
nonregular path components and force-added ignored reference inputs
instead of silently omitting them from validation. Removed paths and
ordinary ignored local media remain valid. Isolated Git fixtures cover
each case; fresh validation passed in
1795587450021173745-4186488.

The cache now distinguishes absent and present environment variables,
including an explicitly empty value. Either optional native audio or
render smoke flag requires a fresh validation even when a prior ordinary
pass has an identical source tree. Both flags also participate in the
fingerprint so ordinary and native-enabled results remain distinct.
Native device state can change without source edits; native results are
not reused on a subsequent explicit smoke request. Isolated negative
controls verify that each flag executes a deliberately failing check
instead of reusing an ordinary cached success.

Local path substitutions through root Cargo patch or replace tables are
now rejected, as are nonempty Cargo `paths` overrides and local patches
in either repository Cargo config filename. Local dependencies must use
explicit workspace dependency paths so their architecture role is visible
to the existing edge check. Ordinary aliases, registry and Git dependency
specifications remain supported. Synthetic controls cover forbidden title
and outside paths, both config filenames and a non-path patch. This is a
repository policy gate, not a sandbox for machine-level Cargo configuration
or untrusted external dependency build scripts.

The cache fingerprints documentation compiler flags and executable overrides,
including encoded flags, plus all present Cargo build, target and profile
override variables in deterministic name order. Absent and empty overrides
remain distinct. The transient Cargo jobserver environment is not included.
A synthetic regression exercises additions, changes, removals and ordering.
On the prior validator, a successful ordinary gate followed by
`RUSTDOCFLAGS=--invalid-validation-probe cargo validate` reused the success
(1795606875781646286-160251). The corrected validator ran doctests and
rejected the invalid flag (1795606925003201188-166930).
This does not fingerprint all machine-level Cargo configuration.

The clone scanner now preserves character literals and escaped braces,
raw byte and C strings, and nonoverlapping nested comment delimiters.
It distinguishes lifetime punctuation and function-pointer types from
function declarations and skips nested signature delimiters, including
array lengths and const-generic expressions. Synthetic tests exercise
substantial exact clones containing these constructs. This is still a
conservative lexical check, not a full Rust parser.
